VISIBLE STRENGTHS AND FAILURE BASIS
Both primary combatants remain within the viewport and apart from each other in these sampled images; no definite primary HUD cover or viewport body clipping observed. Heads and antler outlines generally remain above/away from panels. This limited success does not resolve foreground cover, distant detail, or the full craft bar. Rear-on self-hidden faces are reported as pose limits rather than fabricated camera failures. Minute uncertain contacts in 05-000/030, 07-000 and 08-000/030 are disclosed and not counted as proven failures.

ACTIONABLE OBSERVED DEFECTS FOR ROOT
Priority 1; 00-120.png, 00-179.png, 01-179.png, 02-179.png, 05-120.png, 05-179.png
Observed: Human foreground head interrupts the left combatant's lower leg/foot outline.
Needed visible result: Complete combatant lower silhouette clear of foreground human.

Priority 1; 06-060.png, 06-090.png, 06-120.png, 06-179.png, 08-060.png, 08-090.png, 08-120.png, 08-179.png
Observed: Foreground noncombat boars cover parts of right combatant lower body/feet.
Needed visible result: Whole target body and its visible foot outlines remain distinguishable from foreground creatures.

Priority 2; 06-090.png
Observed: Excessive pullback makes target face and foot detail hard to resolve; nearby similar boars confuse actor distinction.
Needed visible result: Both actors clearly readable in the complete frame with body/antler margins retained.

Priority 2; All 54 / all nine pairs
Observed: Owner full craft bar unmet: world clutter, coarse materials, hard shadow/limited atmosphere, plain HUD and weakly integrated effects.
Needed visible result: Coherent creature/world/light/HUD presentation matching the requested reference direction; framing correction alone earns no full-bar PASS.

FULL CRAFT FINDINGS
C1 world/focal hierarchy: FAIL
Dense repeated bright grass and flower stems, mottled yellow-green ground and dirt, and large dark shadows compete with creature legs and faces. The restored meadow and Palworld references organize foliage, ground values and landmarks more deliberately.
Actionable visual direction: Give the combatants a calmer ground/value backdrop and keep foreground bodies out of their feet; preserve a readable focal hierarchy.

C2 creature/material craft: FAIL
Mudsnout has coarse noisy brown/moss mottling. Terrapup has abrupt stone/fur patch boundaries. Stags have broad almost-black chest/limb areas, mint-green neck pieces and chunky cream antler masses with limited tonal integration. Recognizable silhouettes and Terrapup eyes are strengths, but the owner creature-color board has richer organic material grouping and expression.
Actionable visual direction: Improve material grouping, organic transitions and dark-area detail while retaining creature identity and readable silhouettes.

C3 light/atmosphere: FAIL
Hard dark ground shadows, bright yellow-green field and flattened distant landscape do not achieve the richer soft light and layered atmosphere sought by the owner's meadow key-art direction. A single daylight set cannot demonstrate broader weather/day-night craft.
Actionable visual direction: Improve creature fill/separation and landscape depth in the presented fight composition; atmosphere must support focal reading.

C4 HUD/effects/composition: FAIL
Large plain rectangular panels and a flat colored identity square dominate the presentation. Magenta ring and beige radial polygons/dust look weakly integrated with the world; dust washes creature materials. Cropped or HUD-covered noncombat creatures and foreground rocks add distractions.
Actionable visual direction: Finish HUD identity and visual hierarchy, integrate effects with the art direction, and prevent foreground extras from confusing combatant silhouettes.

C5 wide framing/expression: FAIL
Giant pairings often occupy a small central area against a broad busy field and large fixed HUD. Especially 06-090, faces and individual foot shapes cannot be read comfortably from the original whole frame.
Actionable visual direction: Retain body/antler margins while making the actual actors and their expressions the visual focus. An over-wide frame is not sufficient readability proof.

